'''''Bellis sylvestris''''', the '''southern daisy''', is a species of the genus ''[[Bellis]]''. It is a [[perennial plant]] native to central and southern Europe, the Middle East, and north Africa, and grows up to fifteen centimetres (six inches) tall.
The name ''sylvestris'' comes from the word silvestris meaning "living in the woods" in Latin.

==Characteristics==
The southern daisy's creeping [[root]]s are [[rhizome]]s. what we usually call flower is an [[inflorescence]] composed of tens or hundreds of minuscule flowers. Those on the outside are white with a ligule.  Those on the inside are yellow tube-shaped. The plant bears fruits called [[achene]]s.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.botanical-online.com/english/southern_daisy.htm |title=''Bellis sylvestris'' (Southern daisy) |date=19 January 2019}}</ref>

''B. sylvestris'' is morphologically similar to ''[[Bellis pappulosa]]'' but has no scales on the [[Pappus (botany)|pappus]] of the seeds, unlike ''B. pappulosa''.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Calvo |first1=J. |last2=Quintanar |first2=A. |last3=Aedo |first3=C. |title=Typification of four species names of ''Bellis'' (Compositae) |journal=Nordic Journal of Botany |date=2012 |volume=30 |pages=668–670 |doi=10.1111/j.1756-1051.2012.01373.x}}</ref> Moreover, ''B. pappulosa'' is [[diploid]] while ''B. sylvestris'' is [[polyploid]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Fiz |first1=O. |last2=Valcárcel |first2=V. |last3=Vargas |first3=P. |title=Phylogenetic position of Mediterranean Astereae and character evolution of daisies (''Bellis'', Asteraceae) inferred from nrDNA ITS sequences |journal=Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution |date=2002 |volume=25 |pages=157–171 |doi=10.1016/S1055-7903(02)00228-2}}</ref> 
